export const FLOWAY_SKILL_MARKDOWN = '---\nname: floway\ndescription: Configure and test model services in a running local Floway personal gateway, then help connect a coding agent to a verified model. Use for adding a provider (新增 provider), setting up a model service (模型服务), connecting one to Floway (接入 Floway), discovering models, testing Gateway formats, or authorizing Copilot; not for remote or team gateways.\n---\n\n<!-- Managed by Floway. -->\n\n# Floway\n\nUse the installed `scripts/floway` launcher beside this file (on Windows, `scripts/floway.ps1`). It reads local authorization and the current gateway port itself. Never read, print, paste, or send the contents of the private session file. If authorization is unavailable or the launcher is missing, ask the owner to open Floway.app, sign in if needed, then use **Install Floway Skill** under **Quick Start**.\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Run `floway status` and `floway list` to see the current state and avoid duplicates. To the owner, call an Upstream a **model service** or use their word **provider**. Keep `Upstream`, `custom`, and IDs for helper arguments and troubleshooting. Ask only for information still missing.\n2. For a custom model service, obtain its name and API URL. If a key file does not already exist, create a new empty file with exclusive creation and owner-only mode `0600`; never overwrite an existing file. Immediately open it in the owner\'s editor through the host UI or system opener (`open -t` on macOS, Notepad on Windows, `xdg-open` on Linux). Wait for the owner to enter and save the key; check that the file is nonempty and owner-only. Never put the key in a command argument, tool output, or conversation. For Ollama, run `floway create-ollama NAME BASE_URL`.\n3. Before creating a custom service, run `floway probe-custom BASE_URL KEY_FILE [MODEL_ID] [--auth-style=anthropic]`. Prefer a chat model the owner named; otherwise the helper tests the first listed model and reports which one it chose. It sends small streaming requests directly to the upstream\'s four optional text paths: OpenAI Completions, OpenAI Chat Completions, OpenAI Responses, and Anthropic Messages. It uses only a `user` prompt (or plain completions prompt), so lack of `developer` role support cannot make the basic format test fail. Bearer authentication is the default; add `--auth-style=anthropic` only when the service uses `x-api-key` and `anthropic-version`. An `available` result confirms the chosen model and auth style produced text on that upstream path. A failed, empty, or incomplete result is inconclusive about other models, custom paths, and compatibility flags; never call it proof that the provider lacks that format. If no format is confirmed, stop before creating the service and ask the owner to inspect path and authentication settings under **Upstreams** in Floway.app.\n4. Select the confirmed formats and run `floway create-custom NAME BASE_URL KEY_FILE FORMATS [--auth-style=anthropic]`, where `FORMATS` is a comma-separated list of the probe\'s `confirmedFormats` values. Use the same auth style as the successful probe. The four formats are independently selectable; do not claim an untested format for the upstream. The helper\'s default when `FORMATS` is omitted is only OpenAI Chat Completions, retained for older callers. The probe covers one model, so describe that scope when reporting other listed models. If a later agent request using a `developer` role fails while the minimal request passed, keep the format enabled and ask the owner to inspect Floway\'s `rewrite-developer-to-system` setting under **Upstreams** in Floway.app; treat this as a request compatibility issue, not an endpoint discovery result.\n5. The custom helper removes a final full `/v1` or `/v1/` only when the new model service uses Floway\'s default paths, which append `/v1/models` and the selected endpoint paths to the base URL. For example, `/api/v1/` becomes `/api/`, while `/v1beta` stays unchanged. It rejects URL queries and fragments and reports the URL actually saved. If the model service needs custom endpoint paths, ask the owner to configure them under **Upstreams** in Floway.app instead of assuming this normalization applies.\n6. For GitHub Copilot, run `floway copilot-start NAME`. Immediately open its returned verification URL in the owner\'s system browser through the host UI or system opener (`open`, `start`, or `xdg-open`), then show the user code. The owner completes authorization. Run `floway copilot-finish HANDLE`; if authorization is pending, retry the same handle.\n7. `floway create-custom`, `floway create-ollama`, `floway copilot-finish`, and `floway models UPSTREAM_ID` list models; that alone does not prove inference works. After receiving models, choose a chat model (prefer one the user named) and run `floway test-model UPSTREAM_ID MODEL_ID`. This sends short streaming requests as an external client to the local Gateway\'s three Playground formats: OpenAI Responses, OpenAI Chat Completions, and Anthropic Messages. It reuses an API key restricted to the selected model service, or creates one for the test and revokes it afterward. Do not use a temporary test key for an agent\'s lasting configuration.\n8. Report each Gateway format as available, failed, empty, or incomplete. `available` means the Gateway produced text and completed that request through the selected model service; it does not mean the service natively speaks that format. If `keyCleanup` failed, tell the owner the key ID and ask them to delete it under **API Keys** in Floway.app. Report the model service name, enabled state, listed models, test model and results. For an issue requiring the owner, name the relevant section in Floway.app and explain the exact action. Never include provider keys, Gateway keys, or OAuth tokens.\n9. After a model succeeds, offer to connect a coding agent to Floway using that model. Ask which agent to configure unless the owner has already named one; allow Codex, Claude Code, multiple agents, or another named agent. Prefer the verified model even when its name does not match the agent\'s brand. Recommend Codex only when OpenAI Responses succeeded and Claude Code only when Anthropic Messages succeeded. For another agent, determine which of the tested Gateway formats its documented configuration uses before recommending it. A basic text probe does not verify every agent feature.\n10. Before modifying an agent, inspect its current user or project configuration and the format it expects. Show the owner the exact file paths and intended field changes, with the key masked, and get their confirmation. Then run `floway agent-key`. It reuses or creates a persistent key that can access every model service in this personal Floway, writes it to an owner-only local file, and returns the path without printing the key. Do not ask the owner to create, copy, or paste a Gateway key. Read the file only inside the local process that edits the agent configuration; never print its contents or put them in command arguments, tool output, or the conversation. Use an agent-supported file or environment reference when possible; otherwise write the literal value with a local edit script. Back up every config file before editing it, preserve unrelated settings, write through a temporary file, validate the result with the agent, and restore the backup on failure. The earlier `test-model` key is temporary or restricted to one service and is not suitable for this agent configuration. An all-service key does not pin duplicate model IDs to the tested service, so check the agent\'s effective route or use an unambiguous model alias. If the owner must resolve a collision manually, direct them to **Model Aliases** or **Upstreams** in Floway.app and explain the specific change; do not open a browser page. Report the backup path and final configured model after success.\n\nThe helper supports the local personal runtime only. It uses the local control API for configuration and holds Gateway test keys only in memory; it does not print keys. Do not bypass it with raw authenticated `curl` requests. Its Gateway origin is for API traffic and agent configuration, not a user-facing page. Never construct, display, or open a Floway internal URL in a browser. When the owner must act, ask them to use the running Floway.app and name the section and action; focus that app through host controls if available. External provider authorization pages, such as GitHub\'s device verification page, remain part of their separate authorization flow.\n';

